            {
              "type": "reasoning.summary",
              "summary": "**Analyzing confidence in forecasting**\n\nI’m considering how to interpret \"confidence\" as a stated probability and how it isn’t a guarantee. The aim seems to prevent misinterpreting confidence as absolute accuracy. If forecasters have shared independent prior info, their probabilities could combine, but the calibration of their confidence values could be uncertain. I also wonder if all survey outcomes stem from reported confidence. The question asks for a proper Bayesian approach, but I realize I’ll need to use all available data and confidence values to infer a forecast.**Exploring peer report signals**\n\nI'm considering whether peer reports intentionally include enough information to reveal hidden signal counts. I wonder if their design allows for combining report odds to derive known total outcomes. The values, like 0.685715, might be chosen to reflect likelihoods from three observations with different priors. It's possible that analysts have already integrated previous insights. Also, the equal cancellation of A and B at 0.685715 seems deliberate, which raises questions about the thought process behind those numbers.",
